  "summary": "Report what other people said with قَالَ إِنَّ… and أَخْبَرَنِي أَنَّ…, report a question with سَأَلَ, and change the pronouns as in English.",
  "intro": "Layla missed class today, so Daniel tells her on the phone what everyone said. After قَالَ (said) Arabic uses إِنَّ, with i: قَالَ الْأُسْتَاذُ إِنَّ الدَّرْسَ سَهْل (the teacher said that the lesson was easy). After other verbs, such as أَخْبَرَ (told) or سَمِعَ (heard), it uses أَنَّ, with a. The speaker's 'I' becomes 'he' or 'she', as in English, but Arabic keeps the speaker's tense: قَالَتْ إِنَّهَا مَرِيضَة means 'she said that she was ill'.",
  "objectives": [
    "Report a statement with قَالَ إِنَّ… and قَالَتْ إِنَّ…",
    "Use أَنَّ after أَخْبَرَ, سَمِعَ and عَرَفَ",
    "Report a question with سَأَلَ + هَلْ and a promise with وَعَدَ أَنْ",
    "Change the pronoun when you report: أَنَا becomes إِنَّهُ or إِنَّهَا"
  ],
